To locate the modern black leather chair within the specified image, you can use the following Python code:

```python
import Object_Detector_Tool

# Load model and define object labels (use your choice)
model_path = "/path/to/your/model.py"
object_labels = ["modern black leather chair"]

# Execute model
detected_objects = Object_Detector_Tool.execute(
    image="path/to/image.png",
    labels=object_labels + ["sixdrawer dresser in gray"],
    model_size="tiny",  # Adjust model size as needed
    save_object=True,  # Set to True to save detected objects
    saved_image_path="detected_objects"
)

# Extract information
if saved_object:
    detections = detected_objects["detected_objects"]
    object_names = [detected_objects["object_number"][label] for label in object_labels]
    for detection in detections:
        box = detection["bbox"]
        box["score"] = "{} ({}%)"  # Add a custom score format
        object_names.append([detected_objects["object_number"].pop(label) for label in object_labels])

final_result = f"Which object is closer to modern black leather chair: {', '.join(object_names)}"
```

Replace `path/to/your/image.png` with the actual path of the image file. Note that this code assumes the detected objects are saved as a list of dictionaries in `detected_objects`. If they are already saved, they can be retrieved using `detected_objects["detected_objects"]` instead of `detected_objects["object_number"]["baseball"]`. Adjust the model path (`"model_path"`), object labels (`["modern black leather chair"]`), and other settings (`"model_size"`, etc.) as needed.
